WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.210
[MUSIC]

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:15.620
We really emphasize the ways in which
strategy's an ongoing iterative process.

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:19.080
That it is an essential part
of what you do as a leader.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:23.920
Whether you think about what you're doing
when you're doing it as strategy or not.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:28.030
So you're doing strategy all the time
every time you make a decision

00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:29.880
because at the most basic level,

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:33.670
strategy is just about deciding
how to allocate your resources.

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:36.490
So every time you make
a decision about whether to buy

00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:38.860
inputs from one supplier or another.

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:42.080
Or whether to invest in this facility or

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:46.550
to do this marketing plan,
you're doing strategy.

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:50.332
And our fundamental view is it's
better to do that self-consciously and

00:00:50.332 --> 00:00:53.327
to be aware that you're doing
it than to not be aware of it.

00:00:53.327 --> 00:00:58.817
And so, I think we oftentimes could
benefit from demystifying what strategy

00:00:58.817 --> 00:01:03.798
is and just realize that a lot of us and
a lot of different managerial and

00:01:03.798 --> 00:01:07.443
leadership roles are doing
strategy all the time.

00:01:07.443 --> 00:01:11.669
And it's better to be self-reflective
about it then to be doing it blindly.

00:01:11.669 --> 00:01:15.959
[MUSIC]

00:01:15.959 --> 00:01:21.029
A strong leader is somebody who's
almost able to think like a scientist,

00:01:21.029 --> 00:01:26.346
who's constantly able to move back and
forth between the conceptual model or

00:01:26.346 --> 00:01:29.779
the theory of the way they
think the world works and

00:01:29.779 --> 00:01:33.030
the reality that they are encountering.

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:37.120
And then they're able to translate
the experiences that we're having or

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:41.930
that you're having as a group into
a sensible way of thinking about

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:45.400
the world that the rest of the group
can really get on board with.

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:48.880
I think really what leaders
do is they build a community.

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:53.540
The responsibility of a leader is to
think about the group as a whole and

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:55.860
the social group that
they're operating in.

00:01:55.860 --> 00:02:00.380
A manager is usually more concerned
with kind of technical aspects

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:05.330
of getting the job done so
how do I build the most efficient process.

00:02:05.330 --> 00:02:07.295
But people also need
a sense of belonging and

00:02:07.295 --> 00:02:10.480
they need a sense of kind of community and
they need a sense of purpose and

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:12.782
I think really that's what
the leader's goal is.

00:02:12.782 --> 00:02:16.849
[MUSIC]

00:02:16.849 --> 00:02:21.730
You have a plan that you wanna execute and
part of your job as a strategic

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:26.300
leader is really to turn that
plan into coherent action, right?

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:28.850
And part of that is about what
investment decisions you make and

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:30.740
how you allocate your resources.

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:34.030
But a lot of it is also about
how you organize your people.

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:36.210
And the way you structure the incentives,

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:38.570
the way you structure
reporting relationships,

00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:42.620
how much attention you pay to the culture,
what kind of culture that you wanna have.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:45.750
And even at the level of things
that we think of as kind of HR,

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:49.230
like things like recruiting and who it is
that you're gonna bring on to the team.

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:51.405
Those things are all incredibly important.

00:02:51.405 --> 00:02:54.851
Cause if you don't have the alignment
between the way you've set up your

00:02:54.851 --> 00:02:58.017
organization and the strategy
you're trying to accomplish, and

00:02:58.017 --> 00:03:01.545
the key assumptions in that strategy,
you're not gonna be successful.

00:03:01.545 --> 00:03:06.955
[MUSIC]

00:03:06.955 --> 00:03:10.654
I think the core question in strategy
is about when you have an innovation,

00:03:10.654 --> 00:03:13.720
when you have an idea and
you kind of see an opportunity there.

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:19.130
The problem that a lot of
innovators run into is that

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:22.160
they focus almost entirely on
the nature of the opportunity.

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:25.530
They really think about wow this product
is really gonna do something different

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:26.450
for people.

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:29.640
That's not easy to do, right?

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:32.400
It is very hard to come up
with great innovations, but

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:37.740
actually the harder part and I think that
we really emphasize in this course is,

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:39.470
it's not enough to come
up with the innovation.

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:41.080
You also have to come up with a plan for

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:43.290
how you're gonna profit
from that innovation.

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:45.410
And that is oftentimes
much more difficult.

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:48.625
And part of that in balls really
recognizing what the obstacles

00:03:48.625 --> 00:03:49.980
are that stand in your way.

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:53.750
And those can be obstacles in terms
of powerful buyers or suppliers.

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:58.470
They can be obstacles in terms of rivals
or potential entrance into the industry.

00:03:58.470 --> 00:03:59.560
How do you build a plan?

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:03.700
How do you build a strategy that's gonna
allow you to overcome those obstacles?

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:06.933
Because oftentimes, you come to
the table with this great insight about

00:04:06.933 --> 00:04:08.789
an innovation that's gonna have value.

00:04:08.789 --> 00:04:12.293
And there's a lot of work in getting
to that point, and we'd really hate

00:04:12.293 --> 00:04:16.141
to see the work that you've done up until
that point go to waste because you never

00:04:16.141 --> 00:04:19.669
figured out how to actually capture
the value that you're generating.

00:04:19.669 --> 00:04:24.313
[MUSIC]

00:04:24.313 --> 00:04:26.946
So I think,
when we think about leadership,

00:04:26.946 --> 00:04:30.000
we tend to think about
particular individuals.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.070
So we think about Gandhi or
Martin Luther King, or Steve Jobs,

00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:39.920
or these great figures that we
all think of as role models.

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:42.510
And I think it's important to learn
lessons from people like that, but

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:45.610
I think it's also important to
remember that you are who you are.

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:48.950
And that you have your own personality
that you bring to the table, and

00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:51.280
your own strengths and
your own weaknesses.

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:54.790
And that effective leaders are ones who
recognize what their strengths are, and

00:04:54.790 --> 00:04:59.990
recognize what their weaknesses are, and
build their leadership style around that.

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:02.570
And it's really important
to have alignment between

00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:06.510
what your leadership style is, and the
kind of organization that you build, and

00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:08.940
the kind of strategy that you pursue.

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:14.490
I think we to much too great of
an extent think about leaders as

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:16.070
something that is innate.

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:18.750
I think everybody has
leadership potential and

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:21.560
it is something that they
can develop over time.

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:27.340
I think it could be learned
by turning to others and

00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:31.381
asking them for in-depth and

00:05:31.381 --> 00:05:37.890
brutally honest of assessments of
what your strengths and weakness are.

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:42.267
I don't thing there's any doubt though
that everybody has the potential to be

00:05:42.267 --> 00:05:45.792
a powerful leader in the right
situation and at the right time.

00:05:45.792 --> 00:05:51.344
[MUSIC]

